# .gitignore
# PURPOSE: Tell Git which files to NEVER track. This protects secrets and keeps
# the repo small by excluding generated/installed files.
# INTENDED OUTPUT LINK: prevents leaking credentials (.env) and committing huge
# folders (node_modules) — the two most common repo mistakes.
#
# CRITICAL SYNTAX NOTE (this is what was broken before):
# .gitignore only treats "#" as a comment when it is the FIRST character of the
# line. There is NO support for trailing comments. A line written as
#     node_modules/   # Installed packages
# is therefore read as the literal pattern "node_modules/   # Installed packages",
# which matches no file on disk — so the rule silently does nothing. The previous
# version of this file used trailing comments on every rule, which meant NOTHING
# was ignored, including .env. Every comment below is on its own line for that
# reason. Verify with:  git check-ignore -v .env

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dependencies
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Installed packages: regenerated by `npm install`, huge, and machine-specific.
# NOTE: package-lock.json is deliberately NOT ignored — the CI workflow runs
# `npm ci`, which fails without it.
node_modules/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment / secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The real environment files hold DATABASE_URL, JWT_SECRET, ADMIN_JWT_SECRET and
# the SMTP password. They must never reach GitHub.
# The pattern is deliberately broad (.env plus any suffix), with one exception
# re-included below.
.env
.env.*

# ...except the committed template, which contains placeholders only and is what
# a new developer copies to backend/.env. A "!" line re-includes a path that an
# earlier pattern excluded.
!.env.example

# Backend-specific location, listed explicitly because loadEnv.js reads
# backend/.env first and the repo-root .env only as a fallback.
backend/.env

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output and caches
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vite's generated static build. The Pages workflow builds it in CI, so a
# committed copy would only ever be stale.
frontend/dist/

# Vite's local dependency cache.
frontend/node_modules/.vite/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime user content
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Profile photos written by services/photos.js when a user presses "Cambiar foto".
# This is user data, not source code — and on Render the folder is wiped on every
# redeploy anyway.
# IMPORTANT: this does NOT affect frontend/public/logo.png, which is a different
# path and MUST be committed for the brand mark to appear on the deployed site.
backend/uploads/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Any log file created at runtime.
*.log

# npm's crash reports.
npm-debug.log*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor and OS clutter
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS folder metadata.
.DS_Store

# Windows thumbnail cache.
Thumbs.db

# VS Code workspace settings (personal, not project-wide).
.vscode/

# JetBrains project files.
.idea/
